USER #!/usr/bin/env python3
USER """Emit a custody/v1 record (an in-toto Statement) for a job that reached a terminal state.
USER 
USER WHY THIS EXISTS. A Plumbline close writes the §5 records, the packet's end-of-job facts and the run-log row. None
USER of that can be read by someone outside Plumbline: it is Plumbline's own shape, checked against a corpus a stranger
USER does not have. custody/v1 (evaluatordriven/custody, SPEC.md section 4) is the public shape for the same facts —
USER who set the bar and when, who approved that exact bar, who made the work, who read it, beside which controls, and
USER what the approver decided. This emitter writes that record from the job's own files, so a stranger with only the
USER public checker (custody checker/check.py) and the files can re-read the job.
USER 
USER WHAT IT WILL NOT DO.
USER   - It never fabricates. Every digest is computed here from a file that exists; a fact Plumbline has no source
USER     for is left null/absent and NAMED in `x-plumbline.gaps` with the reason, and the exit code says so.
USER   - It never infers the disposition from the verdict. disposition.state is the packet's `disposition_state`
USER     (written by `plumbline_job.py close --disposition`). Plumbline's verdict rides only in x-plumbline.verdict.
USER   - It never writes a remedy. A remedy appears (under x-plumbline.remedy) only when the custody inputs name the
USER     evaluator evidence file it came from AND that file contains it verbatim.
USER   - It adds no key outside custody section 4 except under `x-` (SPEC section 4: producer fields only under x-).
USER   - It never hard-codes the level. levelClaimed, spread, run numbers, evaluators and harness logs all come
USER     from the inputs, so a CL2 record (spread, run >= 2, a different-family evaluator) needs no code change.
USER   - It needs no corpus, no ~/.hermes and no credentials: stdlib only.
USER   - It never writes its inputs (T7): a harness log is read, never rewritten.
USER 
USER SPEC VERSION. predicate.specVersion is the version of the custody document the job attests: the checkout
USER named by `inputs/custody.json: custody_spec`, whose SPEC.md `**Version X.Y.Z**` line is read, digested and
USER recorded under x-plumbline.spec_version {source, path, sha256, origin}. A job whose artifact is not a
USER custody tree carries `--spec-version X.Y.Z` (origin "--spec-version"). With neither, or with a document
USER that cannot be read or declares no version, the emitter REFUSES (kind `spec-version`, exit 3) and writes
USER nothing: it never falls back to the flag, to a literal, or to a guess. If both are given and disagree, it
USER refuses rather than choose.
USER 
USER HAND TIMES. A time in the record whose only source is a hand is a HAND TIME: a ledger line (inputs/
USER custody-events.jsonl) with `writer: "hand"` or with no `writer` at all, a time typed into inputs/custody.json with
USER no ledger line behind it, or a server-set time from a harness log call with no logger `sent_at`/`received_at` (the
USER response `Date` header or its `created` epoch). The emitter REFUSES hand times by default: exit 3, kind
USER `hand-time`, every field named with its file:line, nothing written. `--allow-hand-times` writes the record, lists
USER each hand time in `x-plumbline.hand_times[]` and records the allowance in `x-plumbline.allowances.hand_times`. The
USER switch FLAGS hand times; it never verifies them, and it silences nothing else. It cannot admit a model evaluator's
USER check time at all: a call carrying neither a logger `received_at` nor a response `Date` header leaves only the
USER response `created` epoch, a machine's clock the logger never stamped, and that is refused (kind `check-time`) even
USER under the switch.
USER 
USER RELAYS ARE NOT APPROVALS. A `relay` ledger line is a report of something said elsewhere, never an approval. A
USER buyer approval whose only evidence is a relay line (no `approve --role buyer` ledger line and no buyer entry in
USER inputs/custody.json) is refused with kind `approval`, naming `buyer` (F1f): the record would otherwise count a
USER report as the buyer's consent.
USER 
USER SOURCES, in order of authority:
USER   packet.json                       job_id, terminal_state, outcome, disposition_state     (Plumbline)
USER   iteration-N-eval-record.json      the final iteration's §5 record: verdict, violations,
USER                                     instrument_in_force (rubric/runner digests), pass list  (Plumbline)
USER   passes/<pass_id>.json             each filed pass: status, channel, provider, model,
USER                                     family, scores, void_cause                              (Plumbline)
USER   inputs/<run>.json                 the run file of that iteration, for the evaluator's
USER                                     verbatim quotes only                                    (Plumbline)
USER   inputs/custody.json               the custody facts Plumbline does not otherwise hold:
USER                                     bar file and its freeze time, approvals (each naming
USER                                     the digest it approved), maker model and method file,
USER                                     evaluators and their model blocks, per-pass time/batch/
USER                                     run/evidence file, controls (case files), harness logs
USER                                     (external), retention, levelClaimed. See
USER                                     docs/custody-emission.md for its shape.     (the Orchestrator's ledger)
USER   inputs/custody-events.jsonl       the same events, stamped live by kit/custody_ledger.py;
USER                                     each time field carries its file:line source under x-plumbline,
USER                                     and generations/evaluations/relays/amendments/spend are carried
USER                                     into x-plumbline with their line sources (F1h)
USER   <harness logs>                    each model call: request, response, headers and the
USER                                     logger's sent_at/received_at; the maker's startedAt and
USER                                     producedAt are the first call's sent_at and the last
USER                                     call's received_at, each with its file:line source
USER 
USER     emit_custody.py --job-dir <job> [--inputs inputs/custody.json] [--out FILE | --out-dir DIR] [--print-only]
USER                     [--allow-hand-times] [--spec-version X.Y.Z]
USER 
USER Default output: <job>/custody/record.custody.json. --out-dir DIR writes DIR/<job_id>.custody.json.
USER Exit: 0 written with no hard gap · 1 written, but a field the custody schema requires had no source (named in
USER x-plumbline.gaps and printed) · 2 refused (no packet, unreadable inputs, the job is not at a terminal state) ·
USER 3 refused because the record would misstate its own history (kit/custody_history.py): a hand-entered time that the
USER harness log contradicts, a hand time with no --allow-hand-times, a relay counted as a buyer approval, events out of
USER order or dated after the close/emission, an endpoint that is a router or a guess (an aggregator call needs a
USER provider pin declared before the seat's first call, every call must be served by that provider alone, and the
USER /api/v1/generation reply must NAME it), an approval by the orchestrating agent or a model, a predicate.specVersion
USER with neither a custody document nor --spec-version behind it, or an emitter tree that is dirty or not the stated
USER revision. A routing request that did not ask OpenRouter for the pin alone with no
USER fallback and ZDR is a hard gap (exit 1), not a refusal: the record is still true, and it shows what was asked.
USER Nothing is written on 2 or 3; an existing record is left as it was.
USER """
